extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Annotations"
	- pagedescription = "Annotations in Tablechart: brackets, trend arrows and breaks in the scale, placed by the data."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Annotations
				p.lead A bracket, a trend arrow or a break in the scale, placed by the data.

	+section("How to add an annotation")
		p Put a #[code div] with the class #[code chart-annotations] inside the chart. Each annotation in it is a #[code span] with #[code data-annotation]. An annotation is placed by the data, so if you change a number, the annotation moves with it.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table …
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Bracket")
		p A bracket shows the change between two bars. If the chart has two bars, no other attributes are needed.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apple juice
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Last season', '251.5'], ['This season', '279.6']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Choose the bars")
		p If the chart has more bars, set #[code data-from] and #[code data-to] to the row numbers. The first row is #[code 1].

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket" data-from="2" data-to="5") +45%

	+section("Trend")
		p A trend arrow runs from the first value to the last. #[code data-at] sets where the callout is along the arrow, from #[code 0] to #[code 1].
		p Set #[code data-years] to the number of years. Tablechart calculates the yearly rate, and shows a warning in the console if it is different from yours. Your number is always the one shown.
		p A #[code span] with the class #[code tablechart-caption] is shown above the callout.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples per tree
					br
					span each
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="trend" data-years="4" data-at="0.46")
						span.tablechart-caption A year
						span +12%

	+section("Break the scale")
		p If one value is much larger than the others, add an #[code axis-break]. Part of the scale is then shown smaller, so the small values have more room. Above the break, bar heights are not to scale, so every bar shows its number.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['North', '12'], ['South', '15'], ['East', '14'], ['West', '140']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="axis-break" data-from="20")

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Attribute
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code data-from
					td Where the smaller part of the scale starts. The baseline if not set.
				tr
					td: code data-to
					td Where it ends. The top of the scale if not set.
				tr
					td: code data-size
					td The share of the height the smaller part gets, as a percentage. 22% for the top, 30% otherwise.
				tr
					td: code data-mark
					td How the break is drawn: two slanted lines by default, or #[code zigzag].
		p A break works on column, line and waterfall charts.

	+section("Annotations on several lines")
		p On a chart with several lines, #[code data-series] sets which line an annotation uses. The first line is #[code 1], and is used if #[code data-series] is not set.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			span(data-annotation="trend" data-series="2" data-years="9") +8%
